If you are talking about skipping the `null` in the set and then passing as not `null` in the revision, that's a pretty small window; as essentially its only one null check immediately after another (since first would fail and not call `.Set`)
```csharp
Collection?.Set(cached);
Revision = Collection?.Revision ?? ContextDisposed();
```

e.g. the current generated C# is
```csharp
IFeatureCollection collection = Collection;
if (collection == null)
{
    ThrowContextDisposed();
}
cached = collection.Get<TFeature>();
if (cached == null)
{
    cached = factory(state);
    IFeatureCollection collection2 = Collection;
    if (collection2 != null)
    {
        collection2.Set(cached);
    }
    IFeatureCollection collection3 = Collection;
    Revision = ((collection3 != null) ? collection3.Revision : ContextDisposed());
}
```
